  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A broken line of strong to severe thunderstorms \nis likely to develop across northwestern Pennsylvania later this\nafternoon, posing a threat of locally damaging wind gusts \nbefore weakening as it moves southeastward late this evening.\n\nThe Erie lake breeze will work in tandem with an approaching \ncold front to provide sfc convergence to initiate convection \nduring the mid to late afternoon hours. A jet max at the base of\nan approaching upper trough will result in 40-45 kts of 0-6 km \nshear by this evening. A few discrete supercells can not be \nruled out, but the predominant storm mode should be linear due \nto the large scale forcing of the cold front. MLCAPE will be as\nhigh as 1500 J/kg before decreasing after sunset. Given the \nstraight hodographs present ahead of the cold front, the tornado\nthreat appears to be low. The vertical profile is also not \nparticularly moist above the boundary layer, so updrafts will \ncontent with dry air entrainment as they become established. \nNonetheless with strong winds at the mid and upper levels \npushing storms along at 35 kts, it wouldn't take much storm \nscale dynamics to push sfc gusts into the 45-50 kts range, \nespecially north/west of Clearfield and prior to sunset, when \nthe boundary layer is still unstable.\n\nStorms will be on a weakening trend as they move southeastward\ninto State College and Altoona late this evening. Much of the\nguidance has the storms falling apart before reaching the Lower\nSusq Valley (maybe a 10 pct chance of holding together). \n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A taste of fall to start the workweek will be \nfollowed by increasing humidity and chances for rain for the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nDewpoints will fall rapidly behind the cold front, from near 70\nthis afternoon to the 40s and 50s by early Monday. The northern\ntier will see some low stratus downwind of the Great Lakes on \nMonday in NW flow. Otherwise, expect clear skies and a pleasant \nSeptember afternoon across much of central PA on Monday. With \nhigh pressure building overhead, radiational cooling will be \neffective Monday night bringing temperatures down into the 40s \nfor much of central PA with patchy river valley fog possible.\nValley fog will be possible again Tuesday night.\n\nReturn flow on the back side of the high pressure system moving\noffshore will bring increasing humidity for the middle and\nlatter part of the week. A slight chance for precip returns to\nthe forecast by mid week, with increasing chances for rain\nby early in the following week as the upper level pattern\nbecomes more amplified.",
